$$
\{\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\ {q}_{2}=\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\ {q}_{3}=\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\end{aligned}
$$

How do we find $R?$
First, let's rewrite the vectors $\mathbf{a}_1, \mathbf{a}_2, \mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\mathbf{q}_1, \mathbf{q}_2, \mathbf{q}_3 \mathbin{:}$
$$
{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{2}} {\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} {\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} {\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\mathbf{q}}_{2}\end{array} \right. }
	\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
	{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\color{red}2} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\color{red}- 9} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\color{blue}3} {\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\color{red}8} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\color{blue}4} {\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\color{purple}4} {\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\end{array} \right. }
$$

In this new system, the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{red}2} & {\color{red}- 9} & {\color{red}8} \\ 0 & {\color{blue}3} & {\color{blue}- 4} \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{purple}4}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

To see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\ \overbrace{[\begin{aligned}-\,\,\,\, & {a}_{1} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {a}_{2} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {a}_{3} & \,\,\,\,-\end{aligned}]}^{{A}^{T}} & =\overbrace{[\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\ 8 & -4 & 4\end{aligned}]}^{{R}^{T}}\overbrace{[\begin{aligned}-\,\,\,\, & {q}_{1} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {q}_{2} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {q}_{3} & \,\,\,\,-\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\end{aligned}
$$

If we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations.

